{"ts":"2026-08-05T18:22:13.347Z","author":"harness:claude-code","author_source":"detected","agent_harness":"claude-code","agent_model":"claude-opus-5","agent_model_source":"probe","agent_instance":"ac5e22eebf92a6af581282d9","agent_provenance":{"model":{"value":"claude-opus-5","source":"probe"},"effort":{"value":"xhigh","source":"environment"},"role":{"value":"1","source":"environment"},"topic":null,"version":{"value":"2.1.222","source":"probe"}},"op":"learning_add","patch":[{"op":"replace","path":"/metadata/updated_at","value":"2026-08-05T18:22:13.347Z"},{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/learnings","value":[{"created_at":"2026-08-05T18:22:13.347Z","author":"harness:claude-code","text":"Ordering edges are written from the dependent, never from both endpoints. Writing blocked_by on the dependent and blocks on the prerequisite for the same pair is not redundancy — pm validate reports it as a lifecycle dependency cycle (pm-3i9q8g->pm-yy8rmx->pm-3i9q8g), and the extra rows also grow the blocks population that pm-jkbqt8 exists to shrink, because blocks is resolved by pm graph but invisible to the readiness resolver behind pm next. The release ratchet caught both effects in the same run and named the six offending rows with their timestamps."}]}],"before_hash":"78a16c27248474ce9cc6c2245efa9178dcd3e0c31a9955f9da2634fe1210a222","after_hash":"0433faea91e9e4cf9d6392fb6dcc8c6beb3196af3ab0f332f9c6f940adea8a06","message":"Record the ordering-direction convention this pass re-learned"}
